        "en": "The four major K-pop agencies (HYBE, SM, JYP, YG) posted their strongest combined quarterly results on record in the first quarter of 2026, with combined revenue surpassing KRW 1.278 trillion. HYBE led with roughly KRW 520 billion, SM narrowed the gap with about KRW 415 billion, and JYP and YG posted approximately KRW 198 billion and KRW 145 billion respectively, growth in line with their relative scale. On an annual basis, the Big 4's combined 2026 revenue is projected to exceed KRW 5.6 trillion, up 19% versus the prior-year forecast, with operating profit expected to surge 45% to KRW 1.17 trillion. For HYBE specifically, BTS's full-group world tour planned for 2026 spans 82 shows, employing North America-centered dynamic pricing and a 360-degree open stage to maximize profitability, while its Weverse platform has achieved annual profitability. However, YG Entertainment faces headwinds as its proprietary artists outside of rights-holding acts BIGBANG and BLACKPINK—Winner, Treasure, and Babymonster—have shown sluggish activity, compounded by legal issues involving a Winner member and a comparatively weaker growth trajectory. In cinema, the domestic theatrical market showed a clear recovery, with second-quarter domestic admissions rising 16% year-on-year to 25 million and box office revenue up 26%, alongside a 5% increase in average ticket price."
